<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Feature 16.2 / Feature 20 — the RELEASE network security posture.

  HTTPS only. No cleartext exception of any kind, for any host.

  WHY THIS FILE IS HAND-WRITTEN: Capacitor 8's `server.cleartext` option emits
  no manifest or network-security change of its own (verified against
  @capacitor/android 8.4.2 — its library manifest is empty and no cleartext
  handling exists in its sources). Android disables all cleartext traffic from
  API 28, so local http:// development needs an explicit exception somewhere.

  WHERE THAT EXCEPTION LIVES: src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml,
  which overrides this file for the debug build type ONLY. Android merges
  build-type resources ahead of src/main, so a debug build gets the permissive
  config and a release build gets this one. A released APK therefore has no
  cleartext exception at all — not by deletion, and not by a runtime check, but
  because the permissive file is never packaged into it.

  FEATURE 20 NOTE ON THE APPROACH THAT DID NOT WORK: the first attempt put the
  exceptions inside a <debug-overrides> block in this single file. Android's own
  lint rejects that — <debug-overrides> accepts only <trust-anchors>, never a
  nested <domain-config> or a cleartext attribute — and `lintVitalRelease`
  failed the build. The build-type resource override is the supported mechanism.

  Kept as two files rather than one, with the drift risk handled by a guard that
  asserts THIS file contains no cleartext permission. That is the property that
  actually matters for a customer's till.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
</network-security-config>
